From 8f78548127d0e4f7c4eae1a42abebfe9c5b962cb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Shaun Adams <s.adams@f5.com>
Date: Mon, 15 Jun 2026 02:33:53 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 28/42] multitenant+transports: VK resolver cache invalidation
 on tenant VK mutate
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Same-replica admin VK mutations no longer wait the full 60s TTL for
the inference-plane resolver cache to flush. Cross-replica
invalidation (Postgres LISTEN/NOTIFY or a versioned header from the
admin API) is a separate follow-on; this only fixes the
single-replica case.

multitenant.CachedVKResolver gains:
- Invalidate(vk) — drops one entry; empty-string no-op; idempotent.
- InvalidateAll() — drops everything (catastrophic-event escape hatch).
- Three new unit tests using a countingResolver harness so the
  invalidate behavior is asserted without time.Sleep games.

Transport wiring:
- New narrow handlers.VKCacheInvalidator interface so handler tests
  can stub the resolver without spinning up the real one.
- TenantVirtualKeyHandler takes an optional invalidator and calls it
  from createVirtualKey (newly-minted VK value), updateVirtualKey
  (existing.Value, captured after the delegate-then-verify lookup),
  and deleteVirtualKey (value captured pre-delete via
  GetVirtualKeyByIDForTenant; skipped if that lookup 404s).
- BifrostHTTPServer.multitenantVKResolver stashes the same resolver
  instance the inference-plane middleware uses; RegisterAPIRoutes
  passes it to the VK handler.
- Four new handler tests assert Invalidate fires exactly once with
  the right value on create/update/delete, and stays silent when a
  delete 404s.

Update path matters most: PUT can flip IsActive=false to deactivate
a VK. Inference enforces IsActive, but the resolver cache returned
the tenant_id before that check, so a stale hit kept routing the
deactivated VK to the runtime for up to 60s — exactly the window an
abused-key revocation needs to close instantly.

Ref: https://jira.f5net.com/browse/XC-25496
